  { value: '95%', caption: "of Black British women don't trust beauty brands selling lye-based relaxers (Level Up, 2022)" },
  { value: '9×', caption: 'more spending on hair care by Black women than any other group' },
];

const DEMANDS = [
  { num: '01', title: 'A safe choice', description: "Make the products safe to use or take them off the shelves. Black women use hair relaxers to avoid discrimination at work and in schools, because they find their hair easier to manage, or simply because they want to. You have a duty of care. If you are not able to provide safe products, they should be removed. No one should risk losing their life, their fertility or have their quality of life negatively impacted from using hair products." },
  { num: '02', title: 'A transparent choice', description: 'Make the nature of the ingredients and possible harms clear on the product label. The correlation with cancer does not come from misuse — it’s a risk consumers are exposed to from using the products as intended. If products remain on shelves, they should clearly state that "chemical hair straighteners have been linked to an increased risk of cancer and fibroids." Easy-to-read labelling. You shouldn’t have to be a chemist to make a transparent choice.' },
  { num: '03', title: 'An informed choice', description: "Invest in independent research about the long-term impact of these ingredients on Black women. Level Up requested safety sheets on 14th March 2022 for just two of your hair relaxers. Once again, we have yet to receive a direct reply from L'Oréal. Help customers make an informed choice." },
